<div class="prose">

<p class="two-texts-lede">Pick one. Or propose your own contested scientific topic: talk to Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Ignash and bring one sentence on why sources disagree about it.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">No idea where to start?</div>
  <p>Pick the three below that annoy, worry, or interest you most. Read one short thing on each. Choose the one you find yourself arguing with. Interest survives four blocks of work; obligation does not.</p>
</div>

<h2>What "contested" means here, and what it does not</h2>

<p><strong>Contested means informed people who have looked at the evidence still disagree</strong>, usually about what it means, what to do, or who carries the cost.</p>

<p>It does not mean a settled question that some people are wrong about. Whether the Earth is warming is not contested. Whether the 2015 Chennai flood was made worse by warming, and by how much, is. The second one is a real question. The first one is a search for someone to argue with, and it will make for a thin Dossier.</p>

<div class="callout warn">
  <div class="callout-title">A good test</div>
  <p>Can you name two people who both know the field well and would still disagree? If the only disagreement you can find is between experts and people who have not read anything, pick something else.</p>
</div>

<h2>Chennai and Tamil Nadu</h2>

<p>Strongest choice if you want sources nobody has pre-digested for you. You can find local reporting, government documents, and people who live with the consequences. Some sources will be in Tamil, and that is an advantage, not a problem.</p>

<h3>1. Pallikaranai marsh: how much should be restored, and what happens to what is built on it?</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> The marsh covered around 5,500 hectares in the 1960s and is under 700 now. Ecologists, the state, developers and residents disagree about how much can realistically be recovered and who absorbs the loss.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Search the Tamil Nadu Forest Department and Care Earth Trust, then a property-development story in The Hindu. The gap between those two is your Dossier.</p>

<h3>2. The 2015 Chennai floods: how much was rain, and how much was planning?</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> Rainfall records, reservoir release decisions, and construction on floodplains all played a part. Serious people weight them very differently, and the weighting decides who is responsible.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Look for an IMD rainfall record, then a news investigation into the Chembarambakkam release. Notice which one contains numbers and which contains blame.</p>

<h3>3. Is desalination the right answer to Chennai's water problem?</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> Desalination plants are expensive, energy-hungry and produce brine. Supporters say the alternative is running out of water. Critics say it funds supply instead of fixing distribution and leakage.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Start with a plant's own project page, then find a water-policy researcher. Ask what each one counts as a cost.</p>

<h3>4. Ennore Creek: whose measurements do you believe?</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> Fly ash and effluent near the thermal plants. Industry monitoring, TNPCB data, and fishing communities' accounts do not agree, and each side has a reason to measure differently.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Find one industry statement and one account from a fishworkers' union. The disagreement is not only about numbers, it is about what gets measured.</p>

<h3>5. River sand mining: a construction necessity or an aquifer being emptied?</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> Sand is needed to build, and its removal changes riverbeds and groundwater. The legal and illegal versions blur together, which makes sources unusually hard to trust.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Compare a construction-industry account with an environmental one, and ask of both: how would this person know?</p>

<h2>India</h2>

<h3>6. The India-based Neutrino Observatory in Theni: good science in the wrong place?</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> Physicists say the site is scientifically necessary. Ecologists and local residents object to tunneling under a protected landscape. Both arguments are made by people who understand their own field well.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Read a physics case for the project and an ecological objection. Notice that neither is lying.</p>

<h3>7. Should India build more nuclear power?</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> A genuine energy debate: low-carbon and reliable, against cost, waste, siting and accident risk. The answer depends on what you compare it to.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Find a government energy projection and an independent critique of the same projection.</p>

<h3>8. Electric vehicles in India: better, accounting for a coal-heavy grid and battery mining?</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> The answer changes depending on where the electricity comes from and where the lithium and cobalt came from. Both supporters and critics can cite real numbers.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Look for a lifecycle emissions study, then check what grid mix it assumed.</p>

<h3>9. Why did the MMR and autism claim survive after it was retracted?</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> The science is settled and the claim is not. This is a question about how information spreads and who repeats it, which is exactly what OPVL is for. Do not treat vaccine safety itself as an open question.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Find the retraction itself, then something that repeated the claim after that date. Your question is never "is this true", it is "how did this travel, and who carried it".</p>

<h2>Global</h2>

<h3>10. The water and energy footprint of AI data centers</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> Estimates vary enormously, companies disclose selectively, and the field is changing faster than the studies. You will also be using AI to research AI, which is worth noticing.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> There is an AP News article in Foundations to start from. Then find a company sustainability report and compare what each chooses to count.</p>

<h3>11. Do forest carbon offsets actually work?</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> Investigative journalists and offset certifiers have reached opposite conclusions about the same projects. This is one of the cleanest examples of two credible sources disagreeing about verifiable facts.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Find a certifier's methodology page and an investigation into offsets. Both are making claims you can check.</p>

<h3>12. Deep-sea mining for battery metals</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> Needed for the energy transition, and it disturbs ecosystems nobody has finished describing. Scientists disagree about how much damage is recoverable.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Compare a mining company's environmental statement with a marine biologist's assessment.</p>

<h3>13. Sunscreen: chemical against mineral, and the reef question</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> Two real debates layered on each other, with cosmetics companies, dermatologists and marine biologists all publishing. Excellent practice on Value and Limitations.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Pick one specific claim, such as oxybenzone and coral, and follow it back to whoever ran the study.</p>

<h3>14. One nutritional supplement, chosen by you</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> Melatonin, ashwagandha, creatine, a vitamin. Industry-funded trials against independent reviews, on a product somebody you know probably takes.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Find the manufacturer's evidence page, then search the same compound in a systematic review.</p>

<h3>15. GM crops: food safety against ecological effect</h3>
<p><strong>Why it is contested.</strong> There is real scientific consensus on food safety and a genuine ongoing argument about ecological and economic effects. Keeping those two apart is most of the work.</p>
<p><strong>Start here.</strong> Notice which question a source is answering before you decide whether it agrees with another one.</p>

<h2>Before you lock it in</h2>

<div class="callout task">
  <div class="callout-title">Three checks</div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Can you find three sources of different types?</strong> One reputable, one weaker, one from an AI. If the weak source does not exist for your topic, the topic will not work.</li>
    <li><strong>Can you say in one sentence why sources disagree?</strong> If you cannot, you have picked a settled question.</li>
    <li><strong>Do you care even slightly?</strong> Four blocks is longer than it sounds.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<p>If a topic is close to something personal, tell Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Ignash privately and you will get an alternative. That is not a special favor and you do not need to explain why.</p>

<p class="text-card-note">Topic locked at Block 4. Dossier handed in at Block 6. Defense Block 7. The assignment is <strong>"S - Unit 0 Source Dossier"</strong> on Google Classroom, open until Friday 28 August.</p>

